## Rationale

Both FindSCC and the Total ReduceEdge benchmark are executed because the total benchmark is composed of two fundamentally different components:
A component that is relatively easy to parallelize

Tarjan’s recursive DFS, which is inherently sequential and very difficult for LLMs to parallelize correctly

Running FindSCC in isolation makes it possible to measure the parallelizable portion independently. This is useful for analyzing the Amdahl’s Law effect, by comparing:

the speedup achieved in the parallelizable FindSCC component, vs.

the limited speedup in the Total ReduceEdge benchmark caused by the sequential Tarjan DFS portion.

This separation helps explain why overall performance gains can remain bounded even when partial parallelization succeeds.
